# LN(1)

## NAME

ln - make a link

## SYNOPSIS

| **ln** [ **-f** ] name1 [ name2 ]

## DESCRIPTION

A link is a directory entry referring to a file; the same file (together with its size, all its
protection information, etc.) may have several links to it. There is no way to distinguish a
link to a file from its original directory entry; any changes in the file are effective
independently of the name by which the file is known.

*Ln* creates a link to an existing file *name1*. If *name2* is given, the link has that name;
otherwise it is placed in the current directory and its name is the last component of *name1*.
If *name2* is a directory, the link is made inside it under the last component of *name1*.

It is forbidden to link to a directory or to link across file systems.

**Note:** the SYNOPSIS above is corrected. v7's page showed no options at all, and the program
has always taken **-f**, which suppresses the refusal to link to a directory. The refusal is
*ln*'s own; the kernel's is what stands behind it, and the two do not say the same thing.
Link(2) here reads

> **if ((ip->i_mode & IFMT) == IFDIR && !suser())**

\- kernel/sys2.c - so linking to a directory is refused to an ordinary user and **permitted to
the super-user.** With **-f**, therefore, root really can make a second name for a directory,
and the result is a filesystem no *fsck* will thank you for: a cycle, or a directory with two
parents and a `..` that can only point at one of them. Use mv(1), which is set-user-id root
precisely so that it can do this correctly.

`or to link across file systems` is the kernel's too - it is **EXDEV** from the same routine -
and it cannot arise on this machine, one EC-5052 drive being the whole of the store.

**Note:** when *name2* is a directory, the constructed name is refused if it will not fit 100
characters. v7 built it with no bound at all.

## DIAGNOSTICS

*Ln* exits 1 on any failure. The first three messages go to the standard **output,** which is
v7's and is inconsistent with its neighbours; only the last goes to the standard error.

**Usage: ln [ -f ] target [ newname ]**

**ln:** *name* **does not exist**

**ln:** *name* **is a directory** - and **-f** was not given.

**ln:** *dir*\&**/**\&*name*\&**:** *name too long*

**ln:** *reason* - from perror(3), when the link(2) itself failed: **File exists** if the new
name is taken, **Permission denied** if the directory it would go in is not writable.

## SEE ALSO

rm(1), cp(1), mv(1), link(2)
